---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/klaviyo-alternative';
const title = 'Klaviyo alternatives for Shopify: repair or replace the working program';
const desc = "Compare the cost of repairing your existing Klaviyo data and flows with reconstructing them elsewhere. Preserve profile meaning, send eligibility and ownership of waiting messages.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should we keep Klaviyo?",
    "answer": "Keep it when maintained Shopify events, useful segments and shared channel workflows solve daily work at an accepted full cost. First repair a misconfigured connector or filter; changing vendors does not repair missing purchase data."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can an export move our flows and predictions?",
    "answer": "Treat an export as data, not executable journeys. Map identifiers and event properties, rebuild rules and templates, and decide what happens to every waiting cohort. Do not assume historical scores or queue positions transfer."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is a separate email and SMS stack cheaper?",
    "answer": "Price the same profiles, sends, destinations and operating work in each arrangement, including overlap and retained services. A specialist may solve a demonstrated channel gap, but it also creates a coordination boundary."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Replacement planning · November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <h2>Start with a repairable defect, not a ranking</h2>
    <p>A useful reason to investigate leaving is an event model your team cannot maintain, repeated manual segment work, an uncovered sender requirement, or a full bill that no longer matches the jobs used. None establishes a universal winner. Keeping working Klaviyo flows preserves trained operators, profile history and one place to coordinate channel decisions. Replacing them can be worthwhile when a simpler maintained model covers the same decisions with less work.</p><p>Write one concrete failure: for example, a repeat customer receives an obsolete cart offer after purchase. Trace the order event and profile identity before blaming the platform. If a missing event or wrong filter explains it, estimate that repair beside the migration. Use the <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo app review</a> for initial evaluation; this page concerns dismantling an existing program.</p>
    <h2>Reconstruct the profile and Shopify event contract</h2>
    <p>The <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080407">Shopify setup guide</a> describes subscriber sync and warns that Shopify-synced profile properties can be overwritten on later syncs. Record which system owns each field. A custom preference written by a capture form should not silently compete with a store tag under the same destination field.</p><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Inventory</th><th>Replacement acceptance record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Profile identity</td><td>Source profile ID, Shopify customer ID, email and phone mapping; merge and anonymous-to-known policy</td></tr><tr><td>Channel eligibility</td><td>Separate email and SMS state, source, disclosure, timestamp and later opt-out evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Events</td><td>Metric name, originating integration, event ID, occurrence time, currency, product/variant and order IDs</td></tr><tr><td>Properties</td><td>Field type, allowed values, missing versus explicit false, writer and timestamp precedence</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Inspect the actual payload used by a live rule, not just its friendly label. Two integrations can produce similarly named purchases. Historical imports, retries and a renamed metric need separate treatment; document what can enter a journey and what is retained only for analysis. Preserve old identifiers in a reconciliation ledger rather than hoping email alone resolves every record.</p>
    <h2>Translate filters and segments as different decisions</h2>
    <p>Klaviyo documents <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115002779051">trigger and profile filters</a> separately. For metric-triggered flows, event filters apply at entry; profile filters are rechecked before actions. A skipped action need not remove the person from every subsequent step. A destination must reproduce your intended behavior, even when its rule names differ.</p><p>For each flow record the precise trigger, re-entry policy, event filters, profile filters, branches, delays, message-level conditions and fallback content. For a cart reminder, specify which purchase cancels which reminder: any order or the relevant checkout, and how a subsequent new cart behaves. Do not substitute a one-time audience exclusion for a check on a waiting message.</p><p>Save the definition and sampled membership of collector, full-price buyer, bargain-sensitive and subscription segments. Rebuild lookback periods, currency and refund treatment before comparing counts. A predictive score with a similar name is not an equivalent model. Retain a simple explicit rule when the new platform cannot reproduce an essential score, and have the operator approve the changed targeting.</p>
    <h2>Choose candidates by the missing job</h2>
    <p><a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> is a candidate to demonstrate an ecommerce email/SMS workflow in one operating surface. <a href="/apps/drip">Drip</a> merits an email workflow demonstration when email automation is the main replacement job. Compare the actual trigger, membership and waiting-message behavior rather than counting automation blocks.</p><p><a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> or <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> can be evaluated for a specific SMS execution, capture or service need while retaining Klaviyo email. This smaller change may preserve more working infrastructure than a complete exit. Confirm connector direction and ownership of suppression and purchase events.</p><p><a href="/apps/mailchimp">Mailchimp</a> offers an audience-centered alternative to examine when campaign production is the priority. <a href="/apps/privy">Privy</a> belongs in a capture-workflow investigation; replacing a popup alone does not replace the lifecycle sender. <a href="/apps/yotpo-email-sms">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</a> is another channel workflow candidate. <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> belongs on an email shortlist only when its demonstrated event and template handling meets the brief. No candidate here inherits Klaviyo data semantics or is prescribed as a default companion.</p>
    <h2>Proposed fixture: collector access and a purchase during a wait</h2>
    <p>This is a proposed controlled exercise, not a merchant case study or observed supplier result. Model a limited art edition with a collector segment and a waitlist. Assign VIP access without a discount if that is the offer; state edition quantity accurately and stop stale scarcity copy when stock changes. Give product education to interested non-collectors instead of sending everyone the same urgency message.</p><ol><li>Create known test profiles for email-only, SMS-only, both, opt-out, unknown preference and merged identities. Snapshot expected segment membership.</li><li>Send a qualifying checkout event, a duplicate retry and an old imported purchase. Expect one intended journey, with historical data excluded from new entry under the written policy.</li><li>While a reminder waits, place the relevant purchase and inspect each queued email and SMS. Then create a new cart to test re-entry separately.</li><li>Change a preference and submit an opt-out before dispatch. Check current eligibility, not the exported state from the start of the exercise.</li><li>Deliver a late event after an outage. Reconcile its ID and age against the replay ledger before deciding whether any message remains useful.</li></ol><p>Record expected and actual event IDs, profile resolution, rule evaluations, timestamps and queue disposition. Agree an acceptable event delay for this journey before the exercise. An audience count matching is insufficient if the wrong waiting message still sends.</p>
    <h2>Transfer queue ownership, not just new signups</h2>
    <p>Inventory waiting recipients by flow version and next action. Choose explicitly whether eligible old entries drain on Klaviyo, are cancelled, or are reconstructed at an approved remaining step. Importing everyone into step one can repeat a welcome or revive an expired coupon. Pause new entries at the handover boundary and retain one dispatch owner for each contact, job and event.</p><p>Route new events through a documented checkpoint. Keep failed deliveries and acknowledgements so a retry is distinguishable from new demand. Use an exclusive bounded cohort for the first live journey only after controlled checks pass; expanding depends on relevant observations, not an arbitrary two-week overlap. A holdout evaluates incremental contribution; it does not prove permission or data mapping.</p><p>Rollback must include events received after the snapshot, newer opt-outs, purchases and sends already completed by the destination. Pause destination dispatch first, reconcile the ledger, then resume only eligible unsent work in the retained system. Keep credentials and a named operator available until that recovery path is accepted.</p>
    <h2>Compare channel billing with a reproducible cost fixture</h2>
    <p>Use <a href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo’s pricing selector</a> for a dated quote with the actual profile count, email volume and mobile mix. Separate billable active profiles from permission to market; billing labels do not establish consent. Check destination, format and message segmentation against the proposed mobile allocation, then include retained integrations, capture tools and support.</p><p>Build three worksheets: current repaired program, migration month with overlap and rebuild labor, and steady replacement. Use identical peak campaign and automation demand plus a larger-list scenario. Get written handling of limits, overages, suppression and service scope. There is no universal price saving asserted here.</p><p>Hypothetical order math: 100 in merchandise sales less 10 discount leaves 90 net sales; less 45 merchandise/fulfillment cost leaves 45 before messaging, returns, labor and software. Do not subtract the discount again. Compare incremental contribution per assigned eligible contact with an agreed holdout; attributed sales alone do not measure the effect of moving platforms. See <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>.</p>
    <h2>Accept the operating program before retiring it</h2>
    <p>Approve a flow-by-flow ledger: equivalent or deliberately changed targeting, permission evidence, template fallback, purchase exit, retry behavior, queue owner and recovery procedure. Essential receipts and subscription service messages need a named sender too; do not stop them as collateral damage when pausing promotional automation.</p><p>The campaign owner should reconcile stock, offer expiry, support exclusions and combined channel pressure before release. Choose frequency and quiet-hour controls for the actual audience, jurisdictions and promise rather than a universal monthly cap. Review <a href="/guides/consent">consent</a>, <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a>, <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel ownership</a> and <a href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a>. Keep Klaviyo when the repair passes and replacement adds no evidenced benefit. Retire it only after the replacement covers the accepted work, not merely after an export succeeds.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
